<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Accuracy (Acc), Character Error Rate (CER), and bad case ratio (Bad) across different models. The best and second-best results are highlighted in <span id="S2.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span> and <span id="S2.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underline</span>, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Proposed Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a two-step approach to optimize token-level preferences from unpaired data (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Data-efficient Targeted Token-level Preference Optimizationfor LLM-based Text-to-Speech" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
First,
we quantify how informative each token is for preference learning.
These weights are then used to guide our TKTO objective.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Targeted Token Weight Estimation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first construct two contrastive language models, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, to capture token-level preferences.
Then, we use the log-ratio between these two models to estimate token-level importance weights.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KTO-based Contrastive LLM Construction.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a KTO-based approach to construct contrastive LLMs.
KTO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ethayarajh et al. (2024)</a></cite> uses unpaired data, enabling more flexible training.
We build two contrastive LLMs, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> favors high- and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> favors low-reward tokens.
We train <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> with normal labels and obtain <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> by simply flipping the labels, treating desirable as undesirable and vice versa.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Token-level Importance Sampling.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We estimate the token’s weight <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025)</a></cite> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}=\exp(\mu\cdot\text{clamp}(\log\frac{\pi^{+}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})}{\pi^{-}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})},L,U))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>clamp</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}=\exp(\mu\cdot\text{clamp}(\log\frac{\pi^{+}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})}{\pi^{-}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})},L,U)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\frac{\pi^{+}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})}{\pi^{-}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\frac{\pi^{+}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})}{\pi^{-}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})}</annotation></semantics></math> estimates the token’s reward <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We clamp this reward between <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> to enhance optimization stability.
For desirable samples, we set <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>; for undesirable ones, we set <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Token-level KTO</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extend KTO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ethayarajh et al. (2024)</a></cite> to the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">token level</em>, proposing Token-level KTO (TKTO) to learn finer-grained token-level signals.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Token-level Reward and Reference.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the reward for each token <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as its log-ratio against a reference policy <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{\theta,t}(x,y)=\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}\!\left(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\right)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}\!\left(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\right)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{\theta,t}(x,y)=\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}\!\left(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\right)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}\!\left(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\right)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle z_{0,t}=\mathrm{KL}\!\Big(\pi_{\theta}\!\left(\cdot\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\right)\,\Big\|\,\pi_{\text{ref}}\!\left(\cdot\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\right)\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z_{0,t}=\mathrm{KL}\!\Big(\pi_{\theta}\!\left(\cdot\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\right)\,\Big\|\,\pi_{\text{ref}}\!\left(\cdot\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\right)\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0,t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed reference baseline estimated across a microbatch (no gradients propagated).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Token-level Value Function.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define each token’s value <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> using a logistic-shaped function:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{t}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{t}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\lambda_{D}\,\sigma\!\big(\beta\,(r_{\theta,t}(x,y)-z_{0,t})\big)\\
\quad\quad\quad\text{if }y\sim y_{\text{desirable}}\mid x,\\[4.0pt]
\lambda_{U}\,\sigma\!\big(\beta\,(z_{0,t}-r_{\theta,t}(x,y))\big)\\
\quad\quad\quad\text{if }y\sim y_{\text{undesirable}}\mid x,\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>desirable</mtext></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>undesirable</mtext></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\lambda_{D}\,\sigma\!\big(\beta\,(r_{\theta,t}(x,y)-z_{0,t})\big)\\
\quad\quad\quad\text{if }y\sim y_{\text{desirable}}\mid x,\\[4.0pt]
\lambda_{U}\,\sigma\!\big(\beta\,(z_{0,t}-r_{\theta,t}(x,y))\big)\\
\quad\quad\quad\text{if }y\sim y_{\text{undesirable}}\mid x,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> controls curvature, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> adjust aversion for desirable and undesirable samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Objective Function.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The total TKTO loss is defined by summing token-level values across the sequence and weighting them by an importance weight <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{\text{TKTO}}\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)}\!\left[-\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}w_{t}\cdot v_{t}(x,y)\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>TKTO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{\text{TKTO}}\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)}\!\left[-\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}w_{t}\cdot v_{t}(x,y)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05799v1/TKTO.svg" id="S4.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/433;" width="476" height="433"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>TKTO (Ours)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05799v1/SFT.svg" id="S4.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/433;" width="476" height="433"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>SFT</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Average log-likelihood for desirable and undesirable tokens during training. TKTO effectively increases only that of desirable tokens.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Text Dataset.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate not only CER but also more challenging cases of ambiguous Japanese pronunciation, we created a Japanese dataset of 5,000 sentences containing the word</p>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">"辛い" using GPT-5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2025b)</a></cite>.
The word can be pronounced either as karai (spicy) or tsurai (hard), depending on the context; we ensured that each pronunciation appears in half of the samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Speech Dataset.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each text sentence, we generate five speech samples for both female and male Japanese speakers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Okamoto et al. (2023)</a></cite> using a TTS model, with training data containing 23 hours of speech for each speaker. Among them, the sample with the correct pronunciation and the lowest CER is selected as a desirable sample. Conversely, the sample with the incorrect pronunciation and the highest CER is selected as an undesirable sample.
The CER is computed using the whisper-v3-large <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use CosyVoice2 (0.5B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al. (2024)</a></cite> fine-tuned on 20K hours of Japanese speech data as our base model and apply our TKTO.
The following baselines are considered:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Preference Optimization (PO) methods:</em>
DPO uses 1.5K paired desirable–undesirable samples;
KTO uses 9K unpaired desirable or undesirable samples;
SFT uses 6K desirable samples.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">TTS models:</em>
Flow matching-based F5-TTS  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite> trained on the 20K hours, w/ and w/o Japanese G2P <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oura et al. (2010)</a></cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Reference industry models:</em>
gpt-4o-mini-tts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2025a)</a></cite> (<span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Coral</span>: female, <span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ash</span>: male) and gemini-2.5-tts-preview <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google (2025)</a></cite> (<span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Zephyr</span>: female, <span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Puck</span>: male).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Objective Metrics:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use CER to evaluate overall robustness
and the bad case ratio (Bad), where samples with CER exceed 0.3.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Subjective Metrics: </h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ the naturalness mean opinion score (NMOS) to evaluate the naturalness.
In addition, we conduct an ABX test.
Please see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Subjective Evaluation Details ‣ Data-efficient Targeted Token-level Preference Optimizationfor LLM-based Text-to-Speech" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for more details.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Objective Evaluation Results</h3>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05799v1/token_reward.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/199;" width="476" height="199"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Token reward analysis. Desirable tokens have a higher reward, while undesirable tokens have a much lower reward, encouraging both weights to increase.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05799v1/weight.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/205;" width="476" height="205" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Case study of token weight estimation. The tokens of target character have higher weights.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05799v1/abx.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/129;" width="476" height="129"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Results for ABX preference test.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:224.3pt;height:28.5pt;vertical-align:-11.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(13.2pt,-1.7pt) scale(1.133200969986,1.133200969986) ;">
<table id="S4.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
