<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Parameter Sensitivity Analysis</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the clamping range controls the scaling of the reward weights, it plays an important role in balancing learning stability and sensitivity.
Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix B Implementation Details ‣ Data-efficient Targeted Token-level Preference Optimizationfor LLM-based Text-to-Speech" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents the clamping range (L,U) sensitivity analysis results.
While performance remains stable for moderate clamping ranges, wide bounds (e.g., (-3, 3)) can slightly lead to accuracy degradation.
This suggests that imposing reasonable constraints on the reward range is beneficial for achieving optimal results.
We select <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2,2)</annotation></semantics></math> as the optimal range for experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
